Why this shape:

- Errors are collected, not raised, so a mod author fixes everything in one round instead of one error per launch.
- The path is built while descending (`prefix + name`, `f"{path}[{i}]"`), which gives exactly the `dependencies[1].version` style you wanted.
- `bool` is rejected for `Int` explicitly: in Python `True` is an `int`, so `"load_order": true` would otherwise pass.
- Unknown fields are errors by default. That catches typos like `"dependecies"`, which would otherwise silently do nothing. If you want manifests to stay loadable in older launcher versions, set `allow_extra=True` on the top-level object and only warn.

For checks that span several fields (no duplicate dependency ids, a mod can't depend on itself), run a few plain `if` statements after `validate_manifest` returned no errors, when you know the types are right.

